Chapter: Identity

 The Identity effect is the right to read and act on the machine's
 identity. Once these two were declared here as effect operations with
 no handler anywhere in the tree, so a call type-checked clean and then
 died at emit -- CDX2034 for a nullary read that no handler had ever
 written. They are performable now: current-user and authenticate are
 real definitions over the identity builtins, so a chapter that holds
 the Identity capability can call them. The effect itself is now a bare
 marker, the way Network is -- the operations live below it as
 definitions, not as handler-dispatched operations.

 To current-user gives the Text label of the active identity -- the
 identity word this process carries, the same one SystemInfo reports --
 or the empty string when there is none.

 To authenticate gives a Text credential token: the active identity,
 but only while the private key is unlocked (key-status), and the empty
 string otherwise. A non-empty answer is therefore proof that the key
 is loaded -- the machine's authentication gate. A cryptographically
 signed token is a richer form and waits on a pinned-key sign
 primitive; it is wanted, not walked back.

 We say:

Section: Effect

  effect Identity where

Section: Operations

  current-user : [Identity] Text
  current-user =
    if identity-whoami 0 == 0 then ""
    else "id:" & show (identity-whoami 0)

  authenticate : [Identity] Text
  authenticate =
    if key-status == 1 then current-user
    else ""
